72
00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:12.100
It's okay if just by chance,

73
00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:14.500
one of those words we
picked at random from

74
00:03:14.500 --> 00:03:16.920
the dictionary happens
to appear in a window,

75
00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:19.240
in a plus-minus
ten-word windows,

76
00:03:19.240 --> 00:03:22.760
say next to the
context word orange.

77
00:03:22.760 --> 00:03:25.920
Then we're going to create a
supervised learning problem,

78
00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:31.440
where the algorithm inputs x
inputs this pair of words,

79
00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:33.520
and then has to predict

80
00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:37.980
the target label to
predict the output Y.

81
00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:40.500
The problem is really
given a pair of

82
00:03:40.500 --> 00:03:43.340
words like orange and juice,

83
00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:44.820
do you think they
appear together?

84
00:03:44.820 --> 00:03:46.300
Do you think I got
these two words by

85
00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:48.100
sampling two words
close to each other?

86
00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:50.375
Or do you think I got them as

87
00:03:50.375 --> 00:03:52.800
one word from the text and

88
00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.455
one word chosen at random
from the dictionary?

89
00:03:55.455 --> 00:03:57.600
Is really to try to distinguish

90
00:03:57.600 --> 00:03:58.960
between these two types of

91
00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:00.620
distributions from which you

92
00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:03.000
might sample a pair of words.

93
00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:06.860
This is how you generate
the training set.

94
00:04:06.860 --> 00:04:09.920
How do you choose Mikolov at

95
00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:12.585
all that recommend
that maybe k is

96
00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:16.285
5-20 for smaller datasets

97
00:04:16.285 --> 00:04:18.520
and if you have a
very large dataset,

98
00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:23.050
then choose k to be
smaller so k=2-5 for

99
00:04:23.050 --> 00:04:28.320
larger datasets
and larger values

100
00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:32.740
of k for smaller datasets.

101
00:04:33.590 --> 00:04:37.780
In this example,
I've just used k=4.

102
00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:41.360
Next, let's describe the
supervised learning model

103
00:04:41.360 --> 00:04:44.440
for learning and
mapping from x-y.

104
00:04:44.440 --> 00:04:46.920
Here was the SoftMax model you

105
00:04:46.920 --> 00:04:49.185
saw from the previous video

106
00:04:49.185 --> 00:04:51.780
and here's the training set we

107
00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:54.360
got from the previous
slide where again,

108
00:04:54.360 --> 00:04:57.120
this is going to be the new
input x and this is going to

109
00:04:57.120 --> 00:05:00.300
be the value of y you're
trying to predict.

110
00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:03.020
To define the model,
I'm going to use this

111
00:05:03.020 --> 00:05:06.105
to denote this with c
for the context word,

112
00:05:06.105 --> 00:05:08.755
this to denote the
possible target word

113
00:05:08.755 --> 00:05:11.840
t and this I'll use
y to denote 01.

114
00:05:11.840 --> 00:05:13.940
This is a context target pair.

115
00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:16.300
What we're going to do

116
00:05:16.300 --> 00:05:19.360
is define a logistic
regression model.

117
00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:21.865
We say that the chance that y=1

118
00:05:21.865 --> 00:05:25.905
given the input c,t pair,

119
00:05:25.905 --> 00:05:27.420
we're going to model this

120
00:05:27.420 --> 00:05:29.520
as basically a logistic
regression model.

121
00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:32.685
But the specific formula we
use is sigmoid applied to

122
00:05:32.685 --> 00:05:38.070
Theta t transpose ec.

123
00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:42.580
The parameters are
similar as before.

124
00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:45.040
You have one parameter
vector Theta

125
00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:47.740
for each possible
target word and

126
00:05:47.740 --> 00:05:49.880
a separate parameter
vector really

127
00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:54.050
the embedding vector for
each possible context word.

128
00:05:54.050 --> 00:05:56.370
We're going to use this formula

129
00:05:56.370 --> 00:06:00.220
to estimate the
probability that y=1.

130
00:06:00.230 --> 00:06:05.470
If you have k examples here.

131
00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:08.295
Then if you can think
of this as having

132
00:06:08.295 --> 00:06:12.450
a k:1 ratio of negative
to positive examples.

133
00:06:12.450 --> 00:06:14.115
For every positive examples,

134
00:06:14.115 --> 00:06:17.400
you will have k
negative examples with

135
00:06:17.400 --> 00:06:21.555
which to train this
logistic regression model.

136
00:06:21.555 --> 00:06:24.525
To draw this as a
neural network,

137
00:06:24.525 --> 00:06:29.830
if the input word is orange,

138
00:06:29.990 --> 00:06:33.825
which is word 6,257,

139
00:06:33.825 --> 00:06:37.140
then what you do is input their

140
00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:41.775
one hot vector passes through E,

141
00:06:41.775 --> 00:06:45.750
do the multiplication to get
the embedding vector 6,257.

142
00:06:45.750 --> 00:06:48.045
Then what you have is really

143
00:06:48.045 --> 00:06:51.915
10,000 possible
logistic regression

144
00:06:51.915 --> 00:06:55.290
classification problems
where one of these

145
00:06:55.290 --> 00:06:59.625
will be the classifier
corresponding to.

146
00:06:59.625 --> 00:07:03.030
Well, is the target word juice

147
00:07:03.030 --> 00:07:05.070
or not. Then there'll
be other words.

148
00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:06.990
For example, there
may be one somewhere

149
00:07:06.990 --> 00:07:09.060
down here which is predicting is

150
00:07:09.060 --> 00:07:11.820
the word king or not
and so on for these

151
00:07:11.820 --> 00:07:15.555
are possible words
in your vocabulary.

152
00:07:15.555 --> 00:07:17.580
Think of this as having

153
00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:21.405
10,000 binary logistic
regression classifiers.

154
00:07:21.405 --> 00:07:23.010
But instead of training all

155
00:07:23.010 --> 00:07:25.185
10,000 of them on
every iteration,

156
00:07:25.185 --> 00:07:27.210
we're only going to
train five of them.

157
00:07:27.210 --> 00:07:28.770
We're going to train the
one corresponding to

158
00:07:28.770 --> 00:07:30.900
the actual target
word we got and then

159
00:07:30.900 --> 00:07:36.030
train four randomly
chosen negative examples,

160
00:07:36.030 --> 00:07:39.630
and this is for the
case where K = 4.

161
00:07:39.630 --> 00:07:46.890
Instead of having one
giant 10,000 way softmax,

162
00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:49.230
which is very
expensive to compute,

163
00:07:49.230 --> 00:07:51.375
we've instead turned it into

164
00:07:51.375 --> 00:07:55.800
10,000 binary
classification problems.

165
00:07:55.800 --> 00:07:57.900
Each of which is quite cheap to

166
00:07:57.900 --> 00:08:00.210
compute and on every iteration,

167
00:08:00.210 --> 00:08:02.820
we're only going to
train five of them,

168
00:08:02.820 --> 00:08:05.250
or more generally, k+1 of them,

169
00:08:05.250 --> 00:08:06.660
with k negative examples and

170
00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:08.610
one positive examples and this

171
00:08:08.610 --> 00:08:10.635
is why the computational
cost of this algorithm

172
00:08:10.635 --> 00:08:12.075
is much lower because you're

173
00:08:12.075 --> 00:08:18.165
updating k+1 binary
classification problems,

174
00:08:18.165 --> 00:08:21.660
which is relatively cheap
to do on every iteration,

175
00:08:21.660 --> 00:08:26.175
rather than updating a 10,000
way softmax classifier.

176
00:08:26.175 --> 00:08:27.795
This technique is called

177
00:08:27.795 --> 00:08:29.760
negative sampling
because what you're

178
00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:33.015
doing is you had a
positive example,

179
00:08:33.015 --> 00:08:34.800
the orange and the juice.

180
00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:36.810
Then you would go
and deliberately

181
00:08:36.810 --> 00:08:39.360
generate a bunch of
negative examples.

182
00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:42.120
We've negative samplings,
hence the negative sampling,

183
00:08:42.120 --> 00:08:47.440
which to train four more of
these binary classifiers,

184
00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:50.505
and on every
iteration you choose

185
00:08:50.505 --> 00:08:53.640
four different random
negative words

186
00:08:53.640 --> 00:08:55.620
with which to train
your algorithm on.

187
00:08:55.620 --> 00:08:57.150
Now, before wrapping up,

188
00:08:57.150 --> 00:08:59.805
one more important detail
of this algorithm is,

189
00:08:59.805 --> 00:09:02.760
how do you choose the
negative examples?

190
00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:05.925
After having chosen the
context word orange,

191
00:09:05.925 --> 00:09:07.620
how do you sample

192
00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:10.990
these words to generate
the negative examples?

193
00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:15.885
One thing you could do is
sample the words in the middle,

194
00:09:15.885 --> 00:09:20.085
the candidate target words.

195
00:09:20.085 --> 00:09:22.590
One thing you could do is
sample it according to

196
00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:26.040
the empirical frequency
of words in your corpus.

197
00:09:26.040 --> 00:09:27.570
Just sample it according

198
00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:30.315
to how often different
words appears.

199
00:09:30.315 --> 00:09:32.820
But the problem with that
is that you end up with

200
00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:35.610
a very high representation
of words like the,

201
00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:37.935
of, and, and so on.

202
00:09:37.935 --> 00:09:40.230
One other extreme
will be let say,

203
00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:42.690
you use one over the vocab_size.

204
00:09:42.690 --> 00:09:45.165
Sample the negative
examples uniformly random.

205
00:09:45.165 --> 00:09:47.715
But that's also very
non representative

206
00:09:47.715 --> 00:09:50.805
of the distribution
of English words.

207
00:09:50.805 --> 00:09:54.660
The authors [inaudible]
reported that empirically

208
00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:56.190
what they found to work best was

209
00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:58.275
to take this heuristic value,

210
00:09:58.275 --> 00:10:00.315
which is a little bit in between

211
00:10:00.315 --> 00:10:02.295
the two extremes of

212
00:10:02.295 --> 00:10:04.785
sampling from the
empirical frequencies,

213
00:10:04.785 --> 00:10:07.230
meaning from whatever is the
observed distribution in

214
00:10:07.230 --> 00:10:09.660
English text to the
uniform distribution,

215
00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:12.465
and what they did
was they sampled

216
00:10:12.465 --> 00:10:15.120
proportional to the frequency

217
00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:18.540
of a word to the
power of three forms.

218
00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:22.740
If f(w_i) is the
observed frequency of

219
00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:24.120
a particular word in

220
00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:26.790
the English language or in
your training set corpus,

221
00:10:26.790 --> 00:10:30.195
then by taking it to
the power of 3/4.

222
00:10:30.195 --> 00:10:34.710
This is somewhere in between
the extreme of taking

223
00:10:34.710 --> 00:10:37.200
uniform distribution and
the other extreme of

224
00:10:37.200 --> 00:10:38.730
just taking whatever was

225
00:10:38.730 --> 00:10:40.905
the observed distribution
in your training set.

226
00:10:40.905 --> 00:10:45.720
I'm not sure this is very
theoretically justified,

227
00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:47.610
but multiple researchers
are now using

228
00:10:47.610 --> 00:10:50.310
this heuristic and it seems
to work decently well.

229
00:10:50.310 --> 00:10:53.070
To summarize, you've
seen how you can

230
00:10:53.070 --> 00:10:55.230
learn word vectors of
a software classified,

